WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.040
Wonder Woman is one of DC's most iconic&nbsp;
heroes, and now, thanks to the success of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.480
her movie franchise, she's as popular as ever.&nbsp;
But the Amazing Amazon's history is longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.830
and stranger than you might think. Here's what&nbsp;
even DC fans might not know about Wonder Woman.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.360
Wonder Woman has held a wide range&nbsp;
of civilian jobs over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:25.440
from military secretary to professional&nbsp;
diplomat, but in Wonder Woman Vol. 2 #73,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.560
she takes on perhaps her most gruelling&nbsp;
role yet — serving tacos for minimum wage.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.440
It all starts when Diana returns from outer&nbsp;
space to find the island of Themyscira and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.680
all of its Amazons mysteriously missing.&nbsp;
Left with no money and no purpose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.600
the Princess has little choice but to find&nbsp;
a job on her own. With no formal college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.720
degree and not much of an actual resume&nbsp;
to speak of, Diana ends up at Taco Whiz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.200
serving the DCU equivalent of Fourth&nbsp;
Meal to long lines of rude customers.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.360
Wonder Woman can do anything, of course,&nbsp;
so she's a perfectly capable food service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.080
employee. But she's even better at catching bad&nbsp;
guys, which is why, by the end of the issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.640
a private detective has given her a side job&nbsp;
beating up various criminals. Soon enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.920
of course, the Amazons return, and&nbsp;
everything goes back to normal.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.800
Most people think of Steve Trevor as Wonder&nbsp;
Woman's faithful, long-standing boyfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.280
but things haven't always been easy for&nbsp;
the pair. At various times in her history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.040
Steve has vanished, died, or&nbsp;
otherwise been preoccupied.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.160
"I wish we had more time."

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.320
"What? What are you saying?"

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.600
"I love you."

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.680
It was during one such Steveless era&nbsp;
that Wonder Woman met Buddy Baker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:40.080
the D-list hero known as Animal Man. Wonder&nbsp;
Woman Vol. 1 #267 sees them meet in costume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.760
with Animal Man having to remind&nbsp;
Wonder Woman who he actually is.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.040
They subsequently team up to take&nbsp;
on an international crime cartel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.560
and Diana realizes Buddy isn't the&nbsp;
goofball she first took him for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.240
On the splash page of issue #268, the heroes are&nbsp;
decidedly out of costume on a beach in France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.040
with Buddy rubbing sunscreen on the bikini-clad&nbsp;
Amazon's back. It wouldn't be long before Animal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.360
Man got married and had kids, while Wonder&nbsp;
Woman was rebooted by Crisis on Infinite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.160
Earths. But for a brief spell in Marseilles in&nbsp;
1980, they clearly shared something special.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.840
DC's Silver Age comics are notoriously weird, but&nbsp;
Wonder Woman's might be the strangest of them all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:21.280
For these stories, writer Bob Kanigher and artist&nbsp;
Ross Andru basically went with whatever popped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.440
into their heads. To start with, the adult Wonder&nbsp;
Woman shared her book with her teenage self,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.440
Wonder Girl, and her toddler self, Wonder Tot. In&nbsp;
fact, the trio often appeared in stories together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.760
with only the vaguest explanations&nbsp;
of how that was actually possible.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:41.440
Then there's the Glop, who showed up in&nbsp;
Wonder Woman Vol. 1 #151. He's described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.080
as a teenager himself, although it was kinda&nbsp;
hard to tell, considering he was just a giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.720
pile of slime from outer space. But when he&nbsp;
absorbed a bunch of rock 'n' roll records,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.080
he started singing his own kooky&nbsp;
songs — mostly about how much he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.760
wanted to date Wonder Girl. It was&nbsp;
a pretty unsettling proposition;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.920
fortunately, the whole thing turned out to be&nbsp;
a dream. But hey, that's the '60s, for you.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.040
Of course, this being comics, that&nbsp;
didn't stop the Glop from making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.800
further appearances over the years. Because&nbsp;
why would you waste a character like this?

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:15.760
Wonder Woman Vol. 1 #170 features another Silver&nbsp;
Age adventure by Kanigher and Andru, and yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.880
that means another unsettling suitor from outer&nbsp;
space. This time it's an intelligent alien gorilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.640
who wants to make Wonder Woman his queen, and uses&nbsp;
a raygun to change her into a gorilla as well.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.280
Diana talks her way back into&nbsp;
humanity by explaining that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.480
as a human, she's an Amazon of mythical beauty&nbsp;
and grace, but as a gorilla she's just like any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.440
other female gorilla. Then she tricks the space&nbsp;
gorilla into using the gorilla ray on himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.960
making him a human man. That enables her to use&nbsp;
her magic lasso, which doesn't work on animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:51.120
to compel him to leave Earth in his flying saucer.&nbsp;
And so ends another day in DC's Silver Age.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.880
In All-Star Comics #13, Nazi agents&nbsp;
capture the Justice Society of America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.040
placing each of them inside a small&nbsp;
rocket and launching them into space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.880
Each hero ends up on a different planet&nbsp;
in the solar system, and naturally all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:10.160
of those planets are home to intelligent life.&nbsp;
Starman assists the sentient robots of Jupiter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.400
Johnny Thunder helps out the giant&nbsp;
spiders of Mercury, and so on and so on.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.320
Wonder Woman's chapter was crafted by her&nbsp;
creators, William Marston and Harry Peter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.920
so naturally the Amazing Amazon finds herself on&nbsp;
Venus, the matriarchal planet of love. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.560
just before Wonder Woman arrived, Venus was&nbsp;
invaded by hulking patriarchal "Meteor Men."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.800
None of the Venusians are prepared to fight&nbsp;
them, but Wonder Woman takes them down easily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.080
and is hailed as the savior of Venus. Queen Desira&nbsp;
of Venus promises to return the favor whenever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.720
she can, and goes on to become a recurring&nbsp;
character in Wonder Woman's own line of comics.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.040
Steve Trevor's an okay guy, but it's kind&nbsp;
of a shame he never really got to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.440
superhero himself. Except, of course, that&nbsp;
he totally did! In Wonder Woman Vol. 1 #174,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.760
Steve becomes the Patriot, a superhero&nbsp;
of exceptional strength and speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:02.640
whose All-American costume immediately brings&nbsp;
to mind a certain other hero named Steve.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.560
It's a good thing the Patriot is here to save&nbsp;
the day, because by some strange coincidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:10.720
Wonder Woman loses her powers at the same&nbsp;
time. Of course, it's not a coincidence at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.960
all. This is all a plot by the Angle Man,&nbsp;
Wonder Woman's greatest Silver Age nemesis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:20.640
His latest angle — sorry — is to get Wonder Woman&nbsp;
to retire by simultaneously stealing her powers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.800
and making her feel like Steve has replaced&nbsp;
her. Wonder Woman doesn't give up so easily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.360
however, and soon enough everything is back in&nbsp;
its right place. Even Steve himself comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:34.701
realise that he's far more comfortable in his&nbsp;
army uniform than spandex and buccaneer boots.

00:05:36.549 --> 00:05:43.040
"Warriors, come out to play!"

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.560
Walter Hill's The Warriors is a cult classic&nbsp;
now, but in 1979 it was a fresh and daring pop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:52.240
culture innovation. And anyone who had&nbsp;
seen it would recognize the shirtless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.880
vest-clad trio on the cover&nbsp;
of Wonder Woman Vol. 1 #262.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.920
It's obvious that writer Gerry Conway and&nbsp;
artist Jose Delbo had just seen the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.920
and couldn't resist referencing it in their&nbsp;
comic. The Warriors only appear for a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.480
pages of the actual story, however, in which&nbsp;
Wonder Woman teaches them a rough lesson about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:15.200
how crime doesn't pay. It's hard to say if Conway&nbsp;
was a fan of the movie or had a problem with its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.440
glamorization of street gangs and was simply&nbsp;
trying to make a point. Either way, it's fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.320
to see a Bronze Age comic deal so directly with&nbsp;
what was going on in other media at the time.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:30.000
It seems like every super-strong hero ends up&nbsp;
in an alien gladiatorial arena sooner or later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.440
And it happened to both Wonder Woman and&nbsp;
Supergirl in Wonder Woman Vol. 1 #177.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:40.640
In this comic, an armored alien conqueror&nbsp;
named Klamos has captured powerful women from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.640
all over the cosmos in his hunt for&nbsp;
a bride — but none are as powerful as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:48.240
the Amazon and the Kryptonian&nbsp;
he found on Earth. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.800
they don't want to fight each other, but Klamos&nbsp;
is ready to destroy the Earth if they refuse.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:56.560
So the duo performs some pretty impressive&nbsp;
wrestling moves with each other — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:00.880
it's all a setup for Supergirl to hurl Wonder&nbsp;
Woman into the royal box where Klamos and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:05.360
ever-present chamberlain Grok are watching.&nbsp;
Diana hurls Grok back into the arena, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:10.640
Kara reveals that he uses a wrist computer to&nbsp;
control Klamos, who's nothing more than a robot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:14.640
A whole galaxy is freed from tyranny,&nbsp;
and the two heroes return home to Earth.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:21.600
Wonder Woman Vol. 1 #185 remains a particularly&nbsp;
infamous story — one that hails from the period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:26.080
in which Wonder Woman had given up her costume&nbsp;
and powers to become a stylish globetrotting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.240
adventurer. She also owned her own fashion&nbsp;
boutique in New York City, 'cos why not?

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.160
Returning to her shop one evening,&nbsp;
Diana finds a teenage girl named Cathy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:39.120
hiding in fear from a group she refers to&nbsp;
only as "Them!" Cathy turns out to be on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:44.320
the run from a trio of lesbian stereotypes, who&nbsp;
want to keep her as a collared slave. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.320
words like "gay" or "lesbian" are never&nbsp;
used in the comic, but it's not hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:52.800
read between the lines. Obviously, it's pretty&nbsp;
gross how this comic portrays queer people.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.720
Naturally, Wonder Woman makes short work&nbsp;
of "Them," and, thankfully, the next time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.280
gay characters appear in Wonder Woman comics,&nbsp;
it's under far more positive circumstances.

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:07.200
By 1973, Wonder Woman was back in costume&nbsp;
and had reunited with the Amazons. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:10.560
comics started to take more interest&nbsp;
in Black characters, although it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:15.360
worth pointing out that they were often the&nbsp;
somewhat clumsy creations of white writers.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:19.760
So it can't have been surprising in Wonder&nbsp;
Woman #204, when a heavily armored warrior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:24.800
woman removes her helmet to reveal herself as&nbsp;
Black, declaring herself to be Nubia, Wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:30.160
Woman of the Floating Island. Nubia was meant as&nbsp;
a sort of opposite to Diana. Her breastplate was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:35.120
adorned with a lion instead of an eagle. Instead&nbsp;
of a magic lasso, she had a magic sword. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:40.080
instead of the princess of an island of women,&nbsp;
she ruled an island that was otherwise all men.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.400
Queen Hippolyta seems to recognize&nbsp;
Nubia, and soon enough reveals the truth:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.160
that Diana wasn't the only daughter&nbsp;
she'd sculpted from clay. She had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:53.520
formed another child of darker clay, Nubia, who&nbsp;
was stolen as an infant by the evil god of war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:57.760
Mars. Mars had raised Nubia to be his&nbsp;
weapon against the peace-loving Amazons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:02.400
but Diana frees her from his influence&nbsp;
and the two become friends. Nubia,in turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:06.560
spreads the message of peace to the men of&nbsp;
her island, and everything turns out alright.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:11.840
Nubia was DC Comics' first Black heroine, and&nbsp;
she's reappeared numerous times over the years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:17.200
She's even getting her own graphic novel in 2021,&nbsp;
from writer L.L. McKinney and artist Robyn Smith.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.880
You might remember how Princess Diana lost the&nbsp;
Wonder Woman title in the '90s to an Amazon named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:28.800
Artemis. It was after Superman died and Batman got&nbsp;
his back broken, so being replaced was kind of in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:34.160
vogue for DC heroes. But it wasn't the first time&nbsp;
a red-haired Amazon had stolen Wonder Woman's job.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:41.760
Wonder Woman Vol. 1 #250-251 told a very similar&nbsp;
story, in which an upstart Amazon named Orana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.400
calls for a new tournament on Paradise Island&nbsp;
— and wins the right to become Wonder Woman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:50.080
Although she's supposed to stay with the&nbsp;
other Amazons, Diana sneaks back to New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:54.560
York as Diana Prince and keeps an eye on&nbsp;
Orana, who has a hard time getting anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:59.120
to accept that she's a valid Wonder Woman.&nbsp;
She's also not particularly good at the job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:02.960
and ultimately sacrifices her life in&nbsp;
battle against a villain named Warhead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:08.160
Diana returns Orana's body to Paradise Island,&nbsp;
and solemnly resumes the mantle of Wonder Woman.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:13.120
Almost two decades later, the Artemis narrative&nbsp;
was essentially a retelling of the Orana story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:17.600
only longer and with a few more twists and&nbsp;
turns along the way. That's the thing about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:22.240
long-running superhero comics, though — even the&nbsp;
strangest things often happen more than once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:24.560
because the story cycle just keeps on turning.

